WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:06.369
This slide compares three governance controls by the point at which they act.

2
00:00:06.369 --> 00:00:17.851
On the left, a preventive AWS Organizations SCP denies a disallowed API call before a resource can be changed, illustrated by protecting mandatory security logging.

3
00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:28.714
In the middle, a proactive Control Tower control validates a proposed CloudFormation resource against a supported requirement and can block provisioning of an invalid resource.

4
00:00:28.714 --> 00:00:36.588
On the right, a detective AWS Config rule evaluates a deployed resource and marks it noncompliant after the fact.

5
00:00:36.588 --> 00:00:43.559
That finding alone does not undo the change or automatically block it; remediation is another step.

6
00:00:43.559 --> 00:00:50.157
The bottom warning states that SCPs define the maximum allowed permissions but do not grant actions.

7
00:00:50.157 --> 00:00:54.848
It also reminds us that control support varies by Region and resource.

8
00:00:54.848 --> 00:01:06.742
Choose preventive enforcement when an action must never happen, proactive checks when a proposed configuration must be rejected, and detection when ongoing compliance visibility is needed.

9
00:01:12.883 --> 00:01:19.496
This architecture explains why an IAM Allow does not automatically mean an API request succeeds.

10
00:01:19.603 --> 00:01:23.252
Start with the AWS Organizations root in the middle.

11
00:01:23.252 --> 00:01:30.790
It has a FullAWSAccess SCP baseline; that baseline does not itself grant IAM permissions.

12
00:01:30.907 --> 00:01:40.677
Beneath it, the production OU policy denies the EC2 TerminateInstances action, with a tightly controlled administrator exception noted on the slide.

13
00:01:40.795 --> 00:01:48.052
In the member account, the role has an identity policy allowing EC2 start, stop and terminate actions.

14
00:01:48.163 --> 00:01:51.348
Follow those actions into the results at the far right.

15
00:01:51.348 --> 00:01:55.588
Starting and stopping remain allowed if no other control blocks them.

16
00:01:55.699 --> 00:02:02.312
For a request covered by the production OU Deny, terminating is denied despite the IAM Allow.

17
00:02:02.312 --> 00:02:06.010
Do not assume the administrator exception applies to this caller.

18
00:02:06.115 --> 00:02:13.501
The lower-left panel describes a deny-list strategy: keep the broad baseline and add targeted Deny statements.

19
00:02:13.603 --> 00:02:23.193
Next to it, the allow-list strategy needs the required Allow at every applicable hierarchy level: root, organizational units and account.

20
00:02:23.299 --> 00:02:32.463
The management-account panel on the left is an exception: the organization’s SCPs do not restrict that account, although IAM policies still apply.

21
00:02:32.563 --> 00:02:37.075
At the lower right, service-linked roles are not restricted by SCPs.

22
00:02:37.075 --> 00:02:40.026
The root user in a member account is affected.

23
00:02:40.147 --> 00:02:42.314
Return to the complete architecture.

24
00:02:42.314 --> 00:02:46.695
SCPs set maximum permissions; they never grant access.

25
00:02:46.695 --> 00:02:52.121
Evaluate the applicable policy and the actual caller before deciding the result.

26
00:02:58.258 --> 00:03:03.002
This slide shows why a bucket policy Allow can still end in a blocked request.

27
00:03:03.002 --> 00:03:09.665
Keep the whole diagram in view as we follow the request from the external principal to the resource-side guardrail.

28
00:03:09.778 --> 00:03:10.953
Start on the left.

29
00:03:10.953 --> 00:03:18.118
A principal outside the organization sends an S3 GetObject request toward a bucket in a member account.

30
00:03:18.118 --> 00:03:21.932
On the right, the bucket policy allows that external principal.

31
00:03:21.932 --> 00:03:25.630
That Allow is only one part of the authorization decision.

32
00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:27.628
Now look at the RCP shield.

33
00:03:27.628 --> 00:03:35.965
In this example, the resource control policy explicitly denies access when the principal is outside the trusted organization.

34
00:03:35.965 --> 00:03:42.435
The red Blocked result shows the outcome: the explicit Deny wins despite the bucket policy Allow.

35
00:03:42.562 --> 00:03:48.672
Above the shield, follow the existing arrows from Root through Workloads OU to the member account.

36
00:03:48.672 --> 00:03:51.056
They show the policy inheritance path.

37
00:03:51.056 --> 00:03:54.523
The two lines below the diagram compare the boundaries.

38
00:03:54.523 --> 00:03:58.195
SCPs set a ceiling on member-account principals.

39
00:03:58.195 --> 00:04:05.103
RCPs set a resource-side ceiling on supported resources, including relevant external requests.

40
00:04:05.103 --> 00:04:08.505
The takeaway states that neither policy grants access.

41
00:04:08.505 --> 00:04:15.631
RCPFullAWSAccess is the pass-through baseline; an explicit Deny narrows the permitted scope.

42
00:04:15.631 --> 00:04:20.902
Read the bottom caveat before generalizing: support depends on service and action.

43
00:04:20.902 --> 00:04:26.198
Management-account resources and service-linked-role calls are outside RCP scope.

44
00:04:26.198 --> 00:04:28.195
Return to the complete diagram.

45
00:04:28.195 --> 00:04:32.950
The bucket policy Allow does not override the applicable RCP Deny.
